# Review: SlackPlugin

The Slack API client, discovery, message mapping and the Slack channel backend are built and match the
spec closely. The channel backend (F-6) is the best-tested part: `channel-backend.test.ts` covers posting,
thread replies, DMs, the `missing_scope` reason, mirror dedup and the feed-backend migration. The sync
path has a single test (`sync.test.ts`), which checks the added count, the join/leave filter and progress
reporting, but not cursors, incrementality, failures or name mirroring.

The biggest divergence is in failure handling. The spec says a failed target writes `lastError` and the
operation still returns a result. The code records the error on the binding's `Cursor`, but
`Binding.syncAll` then fails the whole operation with the first failure. Concurrency is
`SYNC_CONCURRENCY = 2` in plugin-connector, not 3. Toasts are shown once per binding, not once per
operation. Channel creation also moved out of the first sync into the connector's `MaterializeSlackTarget`
operation, which runs when a binding is created. The spec's `Integration`/`target.cursor` vocabulary
predates `Connection` and `Cursor`. Most acceptance scenarios have no automated test.

